{"claims":[{"excerpt":"The Acid Rain Program addressed sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from the power sector, but it did not use one shared emissions mechanism.","claim":"Under Title IV, the program addressed both pollutants from the power sector, using distinct emissions mechanisms rather than one shared mechanism.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"EPA’s Acid Rain Program addresses sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from the power sector under Title IV. The sulfur dioxide mechanism applies an aggregate cap and allows trading; one allowance covers one ton. ... The nitrogen oxides component was rate-based for certain coal-fired units, began in 1996 and did not introduce a cap or allowance trading.","narrative":"The Acid Rain Program addressed sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from the power sector, but it did not use one shared emissions mechanism.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source describes different mechanisms for the two pollutants, supporting the claim that the program did not use one shared mechanism.","materiality":"Central framing; faithful to the source."},{"excerpt":"An allowance provides an entry point to the sulfur dioxide system: each covered allowance represents permission for one ton under an aggregate cap with trading.","claim":"In the sulfur dioxide system, each allowance covers one ton under an aggregate cap with trading, and the allowance represents permission to emit that ton.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The sulfur dioxide mechanism applies an aggregate cap and allows trading; one allowance covers one ton.","narrative":"An allowance provides an entry point to the sulfur dioxide system: each covered allowance represents permission for one ton under an aggregate cap with trading.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source supports the one-ton allowance, aggregate cap, and trading. Describing the allowance as per