ing interpretation of the distinct mechanisms described in the source, not a claim about why the rules differed.","materiality":"Reinforces the supported distinction; no material discrepancy."},{"excerpt":"Introduce the Acid Rain Program under Title IV and explain that an allowance in its sulfur dioxide system covers one ton under an aggregate cap.","claim":"The Acid Rain Program is under Title IV, and an allowance in its sulfur dioxide system covers one ton under an aggregate cap.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"EPA’s Acid Rain Program addresses sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from the power sector under Title IV. The sulfur dioxide mechanism applies an aggregate cap and allows trading; one allowance covers one ton.","narrative":"Introduce the Acid Rain Program under Title IV and explain that an allowance in its sulfur dioxide system covers one ton under an aggregate cap.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source states both the Title IV context and the one-ton allowance under an aggregate cap.","materiality":"Faithful summary of the source."},{"excerpt":"Phase I began in 1995 with 263 initial units at 110 plants; substitution or compensating units brought the Phase I total to 445 units.","claim":"Phase I began in 1995 with 263 initial units at 110 plants, and substitution or compensating units brought its total to 445 units.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Its phase I began in 1995 with 263 initial units at 110 plants; substitution or compensating units brought the phase I total to 445 units.","narrative":"Phase I began in 1995 with 263 initial units at 110 plants; substitution or compensating units brought the Phase I total to 445 units.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The figures, chronology, and distinction between units and plants match the source.","materiality":"Material scale details are accurately represented."},{"excerpt":"Phase II began in 2000, and the final cap in 2010 was 8.95 million tons.","claim":"Phase II began in 2000, and the final cap was reached in 2010 at 8.95 million tons.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Phase II began in 2000. The final 2010 cap was 8.95 million tons.","narrative":"Phase II began in 2000, and the final cap in 2010 was 8.95 million tons.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The narrative preserves the dates and cap amount and does not present the cap as actual emissions.","materiality":"Faithful representation of the cap and timeline."},{"excerpt":"Contrast that track with nitrogen oxides regulation for certain coal-fired units: it began in 1996 and was rate-based, without a cap or allowance trading.","claim":"The nitrogen oxides component applied to certain coal-fired units, began in 1996, and used a rate-based approach without a cap or allowance trading.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The nitrogen oxides component was rate-based for certain coal-fired units, began in 1996 and did not introduce a cap or allowance trading.","narrative":"Contrast that track with nitrogen oxides regulation for certain coal-fired units: it began in 1996 and was rate-based, without a cap or allowance trading.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The covered units, start date, rate-based mechanism, and absence of a cap or allowance trading match the source.","materiality":"Faithful description of the contrasting track."},{"excerpt":"The program’s two pollutants therefore had distinct mechanisms and timelines.","claim":"The program used distinct mechanisms and timelines for its two pollutants.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The sulfur dioxide mechanism applies an aggregate cap and allows trading; one allowance covers one ton. ... The nitrogen oxides component was rate-based for certain coal-fired units, began in 1996 and did not introduce 